  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nTornado/Wind Threat This Evening...\n\nA northward lifting warm front could make it as far north as\nOsborne/Mankato/Hebron by this evening. There will be at least a\nlimited tornado threat along and south of this warm front given\nstrong low level wind shear (0-1km SRH 250-350 m2/s2) and \nmarginal instability (500-1500 J/KG). Limiting severe weather \nfactors will be extensive cloud cover limiting overall \ninstability and widespread precipitation shield lacking the \nmore severe conducive discrete convection. A few breaks in the \nclouds across southern Kansas will likely expand into central \nKansas, but it is unlikely that we will see any breaks in the \nclouds across north central Kansas, which will limit instability\nand severe potential. Am not really concerned about hail in \nthis more tropical set up. Our primary tornado threat will be \nnear and south of that warm front (Osborne to Hebron) between \n4-11PM. Given the tropical environment our boundary layer will \nlikely remain unstable for several hours after sunset allowing \nfor tornado threat to persist after dark. Thunderstorms will \nlikely not display very impressive reflectivity signatures, but \ngiven the strong shear and low cloud bases we will have to keep \na close eye on possible velocity rotation signatures. \n\n\nHeavy Rain/Flooding Threat...\n\nWe have been hitting on this heavy rain/flooding threat for\nseveral days. The heaviest/majority of the rain will fall over \nthe next 6 to 12 hours (3-11pm). The rain will first increase \nin intensity across Kansas and then lift north into southern\nNebraska as we head through the evening. The heaviest rain is\nmost likely south of an Osceola, Doniphan, Holdrege line.\nAlong and south of that line we expect widespread 1-3\" rainfall\namounts. However, the bigger concern is the potential for\ntraining thunderstorms resulting in localized very heavy\nrainfall amounts as high as 4-7\". These heaviest amounts will \nbe very limited in area, but of significant impact. Many \nforecast models hint at training storms with localized high end \namounts over 5\". Given the recent heavy rain over the past week \nand saturated soil conditions across much of the area, areas \nthat do realize these highest amounts (4-7\") could see \nconsiderable flash flooding. Anyone along or south of the above \nmentioned line should at least be prepared for the possibility \nof these localized higher end amounts. \n\nThe rain will gradually diminish and end in many areas after\nmidnight. We could see some rain redevelop across mainly Kansas\nduring the day on Thursday, but of less intensity that today.\nTechnically the Flood Watch will continue into Thursday, but\nmay end early across Nebraska zones if current forecast trends \ncontinue. There could be ongoing flooding from tonight that \npersists in some areas for several days. Additional rainfall\namounts on Thursday will be heaviest across Kansas (0.25-1.00\")\nand lightest across Nebraska (generally 0-0.25\").\n\n\nFriday through Wednesday...\n\nNot going to spend much time on this period as the weather\ngenerally becomes quiet with mainly dry and seasonably cool\nconditions. There is good confidence that most days through\nMonday will see highs in the 60s and lows in the upper 40s to\nlower 50s. We do expect slightly warmer conditions Tuesday\nthrough the end of next week (upper 60s - mid 70s) but with \nhigher uncertainty.",
